# Needle

A lightweight command-line file search utility built with Java.

Needle allows a user to search text files for a specified keyword or phrase and identify which files contain matching content.

## Overview

Needle was created as a Java learning project focused on file handling, collections, user input and exception handling.

The application works with local files and directories and searches the contents of text files for user-provided search terms.

## Current Features

- Accept a directory path from the user
- Validate the supplied path
- Search `.txt` files within the selected directory
- Search file contents using a user-provided keyword or phrase
- Perform case-insensitive searches
- Display files containing matching content
- Handle invalid file paths
- Handle file access and input/output errors

## Technologies

- Java
- Java File I/O
- Java Collections Framework
- Object-Oriented Programming

## Concepts Practiced

- Classes and objects
- Methods
- Collections
- `ArrayList` / `List`
- File and directory handling
- Reading files
- User input
- Exception handling
- Input validation
- Command-line applications

## How It Works

The user provides a directory path and a search term.

Needle validates the supplied path and processes the text files in the directory. It reads the contents of the files and checks for the specified search term.

The search is case-insensitive, so differences in capitalization do not prevent a match.

## Example

A user can provide:

```text
Directory: C:\Documents\Notes
Search term: programming

Needle then searches the applicable text files and reports the files containing the term.

## Project Status

Learning and portfolio project.

The current version focuses on basic file searching and Java file-handling concepts.

